===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# An act or expression of [[condemnation]] or [[disapproval]], especially on [[moral]] grounds.
#* {{RQ:Fielding Tom Jones|book=13|chapter=6|url=http://www.gutenberg.org/files/6593/6593-h/6593-h.htm|text=Though a gentle sigh, which stole from the bosom of Nancy, seemed to argue some secret '''disapprobation''' of these sentiments, she did not dare openly to oppose them.}}
#* {{RQ:Austen Pride and Prejudice||22|url=http://www.gutenberg.org/files/42671/42671-h/42671-h.htm|text=Elizabeth would wonder, and probably would blame her; and though her resolution was not to be shaken, her feelings must be hurt by such '''disapprobation'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Mill On Liberty|chapter=IV|url=http://www.gutenberg.org/files/34901/34901-h/34901-h.htm|text=And not only these acts, but the dispositions which lead to them, are properly immoral, and fit subjects of '''disapprobation''' which may rise to abhorrence.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|1921|{{w|D. H. Lawrence}}|{{w|Sea and Sardinia}}|chapter=Chapter I|url=http://gutenberg.net.au/ebooks09/0900021.txt|text=No one seems to think so, however. Yet they view my arrival with a knapsack on my back with cold '''disapprobation''', as unseemly as if I had arrived riding on a pig. I ought to be in a carriage, and the knapsack ought to be a new suitcase.}}

